.TH P9SHARE 8 "2026" "p9share" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
p9share \- share directories between machines over 9P, through ssh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p9share export
.I DIR HOST
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B p9share unexport
.I NAME
.br
.B sudo p9share mount
.I USER NAME DIR
.br
.B sudo p9share unmount
.I NAME
.br
.B p9share
.BR rw | ro | off
.I NAME
.br
.B p9share status
.br
.B sudo p9share wsl\-boot
.br
.B p9share boot
.RB [ \-\-detach ]
.br
.B p9share serve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B p9share
makes a directory on one machine appear on another, with the kernel's 9p client, through an ssh tunnel that the machine with the files
opens.  The receiving machine needs no open port.  Install the same package on both; a machine is an exporter, a receiver or both,
according to what is configured on it.
.SS Exporter (as an ordinary user)
.TP
.BI "export " "DIR HOST " [ NAME ]
Share DIR with HOST (a name as ssh knows it) as NAME, by default the directory's own name.  The line goes in
.IR ~/.config/p9share/exports ;
the running service notices within a few seconds.  Prints the command to run on HOST.
.TP
.BI unexport " NAME"
Stop sharing it.
.SS Receiver
.TP
.BI "mount " "USER NAME DIR"
(root) Show the share NAME of the account USER on this machine (the account the exporter logs in as) at DIR.  The line goes in
.IR /etc/p9share/mounts .
It is mounted while the exporter's tunnel is up and unmounted while it is not.
.TP
.BI unmount " NAME"
(root) Remove it and unmount.
.TP
.BR rw | ro | off " \fINAME\fP"
(the account USER, no sudo) Mounted read-write (the default), read-only, or not mounted.  Follows within a few seconds.
.SS Both
.TP
.B status
What is shared, what is mounted, and whether the daemon runs.
.TP
.B wsl\-boot
(root, WSL2) Put the daemon in the [boot] command of
.IR /etc/wsl.conf ,
which WSL2 has instead of systemd.  A command already there is kept and runs first.
.TP
.B boot
The root daemon, run by the
.B p9share
service (or with
.BR \-\-detach ,
in the background, logging to /var/log/p9share.log).  Starts
.B serve
for every user who has an exports file and makes the mounts of
.I /etc/p9share/mounts
agree with the tunnels and the users' modes, every two seconds.
.TP
.B serve
One user's exports in the foreground: for each, diod (unprivileged, on a private socket under
.IR ~/.local/state/p9share ),
a relay that supplies the numeric uid in the attach message, and
.BR "ssh \-R" ,
which puts the socket at
.I ~/.p9share/NAME.sock
on HOST.  Each is restarted when it dies (waiting 1, 2, 4 ... 30 seconds after quick deaths); ssh runs with ServerAliveInterval 15
and ServerAliveCountMax 3, so a dead link is noticed in about 45 seconds.  The exporter also writes
.I ~/.p9share/NAME.info
on HOST: the user name and directory the mount asks diod for.
.SH SECURITY
Mounts are made nosuid,nodev, with access=any: any user on the receiving machine who can reach the mount point sees the files with the
exporter's rights.  Every value that goes from the exporter's files into a mount option is checked first.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/p9share/exports
.TQ
.I /etc/p9share/mounts
See
.BR p9share\-exports (5)
and
.BR p9share\-mounts (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/p9share/mode.d/NAME
The mode (rw, ro, off) of a mount, written by
.BR "p9share rw|ro|off" .
.TP
.I ~/.p9share/
On the receiving machine: the socket and the .info file of each share.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR diod (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR mount (8)
